# zensim per-stage NEON attribution — 3 kernels were SLOWER than scalar; fixed
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
bench:   cd zensim-bench && cargo bench --bench stage_isolation   (1024x1024)

## Why per-stage

The whole-pipeline bench showed zensim getting only 1.2-1.3x from NEON where
butteraugli — a comparable multi-scale XYB+blur metric — gets 3.4-3.7x on the
same host, and that the shortfall was inside the image kernels rather than in
scoring. Attributing further normally wants a profiler; macOS dtrace
(cargo-flamegraph) needs SIP disabled. Exporting the stage entry points
(`zensim::__bench_stages`, doc(hidden), following the existing
`bench_pu_xyb_*` precedent) gets the same attribution from an A/B instead.

## Per-stage result — most kernels were SLOWER with NEON

| stage                        | NEON     | scalar   | ratio |
|------------------------------|----------|----------|-------|
| simd_ops/abs_diff_sum        |  0.351ms |  0.087ms | 0.25x |
| simd_ops/sq_diff_sum         |  0.351ms |  0.117ms | 0.33x |
| blur/downscale_2x            |  0.149ms |  0.099ms | 0.66x |
| blur/fused_blur_h_ssim       | 10.741ms |  7.526ms | 0.70x |
| simd_ops/sq_sum_into         |  0.100ms |  0.092ms | 0.92x |
| blur/box_blur_1pass          |  1.730ms |  1.762ms | 1.02x |
| simd_ops/mul_into            |  0.099ms |  0.104ms | 1.05x |
| color/srgb_to_positive_xyb   |  2.134ms |  2.951ms | 1.38x |

## Root cause

On AArch64, NEON is BASELINE — so LLVM autovectorises the scalar body anyway.
The hand-written NEON variants are not competing against scalar code, they are
competing against the autovectoriser, and mostly losing.

For the two reductions the mechanism is explicit: `abs_diff_sum_inner` /
`sq_diff_sum_inner` call `reduce_add()` INSIDE the loop, once per 8 elements.
A horizontal reduction per iteration is a serialising cross-lane op and creates
a loop-carried dependency on the accumulator. The autovectorised scalar form
keeps a vector accumulator and reduces once. (The x86 `_v3`/`_v4` variants at
simd_ops.rs:389/418 have the same shape — unmeasured here, no x86 host.)

## FIXED — three kernels, bit-identical, now dispatch to scalar on aarch64

Bit-identity was VERIFIED before switching (differing lanes = 0 on 64K-element
inputs), so these are pure speed changes with no effect on any score:

  abs_diff_sum   0.351 -> 0.089ms   3.9x
  sq_diff_sum    0.351 -> 0.116ms   3.0x
  downscale_2x   0.149 -> 0.098ms   1.5x

Whole-pipeline effect (tier_isolation, same host):

  1920x1080   14.508 -> 13.264ms    8.6% faster
  512x512      2.416 ->  2.397ms    0.8%
  256x256      1.068 ->  1.074ms    ~0

104 lib tests green; the metric is numerically unchanged by construction.

## NOT FIXED — needs a numerics decision from the owner

`fused_blur_h_ssim` is the biggest single opportunity: 10.741ms NEON vs
7.526ms scalar = **1.43x available**, and it is the dominant stage. But it is
NOT bit-identical between the two paths — `mu1`/`mu2` match exactly while
`sigma_sq` and `sigma12` differ on 55426/65536 and 56300/65536 lanes (FMA
association differs between the hand-written and autovectorised forms).

`sq_sum_into` (0.92x) is likewise not bit-identical (10675/65536 lanes).

### RESOLVED — measured the cost, and the crate's own threshold rules it out

Rather than leave this as a judgement call, the score-level impact was measured.
The intermediate-lane divergence is not the thing that matters; the SCORE is.

`fused_blur_h_ssim` was switched to the scalar tier on aarch64 and zensim
scores (ZensimProfile::B) were compared before/after across 3 sizes x 3
distortion levels:

  case                current(NEON)    scalar-tier      delta
  128x128 delta=2     95.661428026     95.661461108     3.31e-05
  128x128 delta=10    90.507352098     90.500504942     6.85e-03
  128x128 delta=30    78.945956683     78.948438528     2.48e-03
  256x256 delta=2     95.671092033     95.663554960     7.54e-03
  256x256 delta=10    90.531155493     90.525126443     6.03e-03
  256x256 delta=30    78.821579502     78.822552416     9.73e-04
  512x512 delta=2     95.568990906     95.558500443     1.05e-02
  512x512 delta=10    90.146993562     90.158889384     1.19e-02
  512x512 delta=30    78.137755759     78.128091802     9.66e-03

  worst score delta = 1.19e-02
  crate tolerance   = 1.00e-02   (zensim/tests/cross_platform.rs::TOLERANCE)

**VERDICT: EXCEEDS the crate's own score tolerance. NOT shipped.**

This is not a deferral — the crate publishes a score-comparison threshold and
the change crosses it, at the largest size tested (and the trend is worse with
size, so 1920x1080 would be worse still). 1.19e-2 on a 0-100 scale is small in
absolute terms, but zensim drives ship/no-ship codec decisions and is a
training target; the owner set 1e-2 and this is over it.

The change is preserved in `git stash` if the threshold is ever revisited. To
re-evaluate: re-run the score-delta comparison above, and note that any
acceptance would also need the affected features' REL_TOLERANCE overrides
re-derived, since sigma_sq/sigma12 feed the SSIM-family features directly.

`sq_sum_into` (0.92x) was not pursued separately: it is also non-bit-identical
(10675/65536 lanes) and its upside is 8%, far below fused_blur's 43%, so it
carries the same numeric risk for a fraction of the gain.

### Note on the v1 byte-identity gate

`zensim/tests/v1_golden_bytes.rs` calls itself "the gate" and says to back out
any refactor that changes a v1 bit. It CANNOT serve as the arbiter here: it
fails on aarch64 regardless — 246/372 (synthetic) and 241/372 (real) features
diverge at HEAD **and** at HEAD~1, i.e. before any change in this work. That
matches the documented design (the golden is a reference-platform x86-64
tripwire, `--skip`ped in the portable matrix). Verified explicitly so the
failure is not mistaken for damage from the three shipped dispatches — those
were bit-identical and changed no feature.
